---
title: Pricing — FrontierJS Supply Co.
description: What a standing order costs, and what each one includes.
render: static
---
<!--
  The price list, prerendered to plain HTML at build time.

  No `publishes:` in the frontmatter, which is a claim: nothing on this page is
  gated. The build CHECKS it rather than taking it — `Plan` reads at 0, so the
  page is allowed to exist. See index.meta.js.

  ── The number in the table is the OLD number ────────────────────────────────

  Every price here was written into this file when the site was built, and the
  shop raises prices without rebuilding — `plans.reprice` closes one window and
  opens another, and nothing in a file knows that happened. That is not a flaw
  in prerendering, it is what prerendering IS, and the honest answer is the
  island: it asks the shop what it charges now and corrects the cells in place.

  It is the same argument the catalogue makes about a product price, one table
  along, and it is sharper here — a subscription price moves once a year rather
  than with a sale, so a stale one looks completely plausible for months.
-->
<script>
  import LivePlans from '../../islands/LivePlans.mesa'
  import { money } from '@/money.js'

  export let data = null

  const plans = data?.plans ?? []

  // What a period is called in a sentence. The INTERVAL is the schema's — a
  // value added to the enum reads as itself here rather than vanishing.
  const per = (interval) => interval === 'yearly' ? 'a year' : 'a month'
</script>

<div class="container stack">
  <header class="stack">
    <h1>Standing orders</h1>
    <p class="text-muted">
      A shelf restocked on a cycle, billed on the same one. Cancel whenever —
      anything already invoiced is still owed, and nothing else is.
    </p>
  </header>

  <ul class="cards" id="plans">
    {#each plans as p}
      <!--
        `data-baked` is the number this file was built with, kept beside the
        rendered one so the island can tell *checked and agreed* from *never
        ran* — which look identical if only a change is recorded.
      -->
      <li class="card" data-plan={p.code}>
        <div class="surface-body stack">
          <h2 class="h4">{p.name}</h2>
          <p class="price" data-baked={p.price ?? ''}>
            {p.price == null ? 'Ask us' : money(p.price)}
            <span class="text-muted text-sm">{per(p.interval)}</span>
          </p>
          {#if p.blurb}<p class="text-sm text-muted">{p.blurb}</p>{/if}
          <a class="btn" href="/account/">Start with an account</a>
        </div>
      </li>
    {/each}
  </ul>

  <LivePlans client:load />

  <section class="stack">
    <h2 class="h4">What happens on the day</h2>
    <ul class="rows divided">
      <li>
        <strong>You are invoiced for the period, in advance.</strong>
        The document is frozen the moment it is issued — number, dates and every
        figure on it — so a correction is a credit note beside it and never an
        edit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Change the plan mid-cycle and only the difference moves.</strong>
        The rest of the period is prorated to the day: an upgrade is invoiced,
        a downgrade is credited.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A price rise does not reach you.</strong>
        A subscription names the price it was sold at, so what the shop charges
        new subscribers has nothing to do with what you pay.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  /*
   * A price is the thing this page exists to say, so it is not a muted chip:
   * the Badge term is a categorical STATUS, which is the wrong vocabulary and
   * the wrong emphasis for a number somebody came here to read.
   *
   * Every value is a token. A `var()` naming one nothing defines drops the
   * whole DECLARATION rather than producing a wrong value, and nothing reports
   * it — which is how a page ends up linked, matched and unstyled line by line
   * (`FJS-545`).
   */
  .price {
    font-size:   var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: 600;
    color:       var(--ink);
    margin:      0;
  }
</style>
